19:T6b5,## Class, the blueprint

A **class** is a *template*. It defines two things: the **fields** an object will hold (its **state**) and the **methods** that act on that state (its **behaviour**). On its own a class stores nothing, it is just a description, like architectural plans for a house. `Car` says “every car HAS a brand and a speed, and CAN accelerate and brake”, but it is not itself a car you can drive.

## Object, a runtime instance

An **object** is what you get when you build from the blueprint. The `new` keyword **allocates** a fresh object in memory and hands you a reference to it:

```
Car tesla = new Car();   // a real, drivable instance
```

That object has its **OWN copy** of every field. Writing `tesla.speed = 0` touches only `tesla`'s memory, nobody else's.

## One class, many objects

From a **single** class you can stamp out as many objects as you like, and each is **independent**. `tesla` and `mini` share the same shape and the same methods, yet `tesla.brand` and `mini.brand` live in separate slots, change one and the other is untouched.

## Identity vs. state

Two objects can hold **equal state** and still be **different instances**. `new Car()` and another `new Car()` with identical fields are *not the same object*, each has its own **identity** (its own address in memory). State is *what an object holds*; identity is *which object it is*.

## Compile-time vs. runtime

The class exists at **compile time**, it is written in your source and fixed before the program runs. Objects come to life at **runtime**, created and destroyed as the program executes. One static `Car` definition; potentially millions of `Car` objects flowing through memory while the app is running.c:["$","div",null,{"className":"flex min-h-screen","children":[["$","$L17",null,{"activeModule":"lld-foundations","activeLesson":"objects"}],["$","$L18","objects",{"lesson":{"id":"objects","patternId":"lld-foundations","title":"Objects & Classes","kind":"concept","difficulty":"Intro","statement":"A class is a blueprint; an object is a concrete instance created from it, with its own state.","companies":["Amazon","Microsoft","Adobe"],"theory":"$19","approaches":[{"label":"Blueprint → instances","code":["class Car {","    String brand;","    int speed;","    void accelerate() { speed += 10; }","}","","Car tesla = new Car();","Car mini  = new Car();"],"steps":[{"view":"class","codeLine":1,"narration":"Start with the blueprint. The Car class defines the fields every car will hold (brand, speed) and the methods it can perform (accelerate, brake), but it stores no actual values. It is a template, not a car.","classDiagram":{"classes":[{"id":"car","name":"Car","fields":["- brand: String","- speed: int"],"methods":["+ accelerate()","+ brake()"],"x":1,"y":0.2},{"id":"tesla","name":"tesla : Car","fields":["brand = \"Tesla\"","speed = 0"],"x":0,"y":1.7,"tint":"accent2"},{"id":"mini","name":"mini : Car","fields":["brand = \"Mini\"","speed = 60"],"x":1.6,"y":1.7,"tint":"accent2"}],"relations":[{"id":"newTesla","from":"tesla","to":"car","kind":"depend","label":"«new»"},{"id":"newMini","from":"mini","to":"car","kind":"depend","label":"«new»"}],"shownClassIds":["car"],"shownRelIds":[],"activeIds":["car"]},"vars":[["class","a blueprint, not an object"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":7,"narration":"new Car() allocates a real object in memory and binds it to tesla. This instance gets its OWN copy of the state: brand = \"Tesla\", speed = 0. The values live in tesla, not in the class.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["car","tesla"],"shownRelIds":["newTesla"],"activeIds":["tesla"]},"vars":[["tesla","instance with its own state"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":8,"narration":"A second new Car() makes mini, a completely separate object from the SAME class, with different values: brand = \"Mini\", speed = 60. Its state is fully independent of tesla's.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["car","tesla","mini"],"shownRelIds":["newTesla"],"activeIds":["mini"]},"vars":[["mini","separate, independent state"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":7,"narration":"Both dashed «new» arrows point back to Car: every object is an INSTANCE OF the class it was built from. The class supplies the shape and behaviour; each object supplies its own values.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["car","tesla","mini"],"shownRelIds":["newTesla","newMini"],"activeIds":["car"]},"vars":[["«new»","instance-of the class"]]},{"view":"class","narration":"One class, many objects. tesla and mini share Car's blueprint yet each has its own identity and its own state, equal-looking cars are still different instances. That is the heart of object-oriented thinking.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["car","tesla","mini"],"shownRelIds":["newTesla","newMini"],"activeIds":[]},"vars":[["identity","which object it is"],["state","what it holds"]]}]}]}}]]}]
